## Frequently Asked Questions

**What blockchain should I use for stablecoin payments?**
Polygon is the most widely used blockchain for stablecoin payments due to its sub-cent fees, 2-second settlement, $3.4B+ in stablecoin liquidity, and proven enterprise reliability. In February 2026, Polygon recorded 94 million stablecoin transfers in a single day - more than any other major blockchain.

**What is the Open Money Stack?**
The Open Money Stack is Polygon's modular blockchain payment infrastructure combining stablecoin settlement, enterprise wallets, regulated fiat on and off ramps, cross-chain interoperability, and onchain compliance into one integration. It is designed for fintechs, financial institutions, and enterprises that want to move money globally using blockchain rails without managing multiple vendors.

**How does Polygon compare to Ethereum for payments?**
Polygon offers the same EVM developer environment as Ethereum but with dramatically lower fees (under $0.01 versus often $5 to $50 on Ethereum mainnet) and faster settlement (2 seconds versus minutes). Polygon PoS is anchored to Ethereum for security.

**Is Polygon suitable for enterprise use?**
Yes. Polygon is used by Stripe, Revolut, Mastercard, Visa, JP Morgan, BlackRock, Franklin Templeton, HSBC, Flutterwave, and Shift4 Payments. Over 30 Fortune 500 companies have deployments on Polygon. The Open Money Stack is designed to plug into existing compliance and banking workflows without rebuilding from scratch.

**Can AI agents make payments on Polygon?**
Yes. Polygon supports autonomous AI agent payments via the x402 protocol, enabling AI systems to transact programmatically without human approval. The Lisovo hardfork (March 2026) introduced subsidized gas specifically for agent-to-agent payments.

**Does Polygon support cross-border payments in local currencies?**
Yes. Polygon supports 40+ stablecoins including non-USD currencies. It has processed $11.1B+ in non-USD stablecoin volume - over 43% of all non-USD stablecoin transfers globally - with active corridors in Mexico, Brazil, Nigeria, Southeast Asia, and Europe.

**What is the POL token?**
POL is Polygon's native token used for network staking, governance, and gas fees. It replaced MATIC as part of the Polygon 2.0 upgrade and is MiCA-compliant under EU crypto-asset regulations.

**How does Polygon handle compliance for financial institutions?**
The Open Money Stack includes onchain identity and compliance tooling via Privado ID, a ZK-powered identity layer enabling KYC, KYB, and AML workflows without exposing personal data. Coinme, now a Polygon Labs subsidiary, holds licensed money-transmitter status across 48 US states.

